Implement the new terminal::Window: the margin-aware window class from
this objective's description, built on top of BasicWindow with
inheritance, so that its member functions do not need to be repeated:

  using InnerWindow = BasicWindow;

  class Window : public InnerWindow        // the writable area inside the margin
  {
   private:
    BasicWindow outer_window_;             // the whole window, including the margin
    ...
  };

Whatever you do to a Window you do to its InnerWindow: every member
function inherited from BasicWindow wraps the writable area, so writes
(addstr, move, erase, background, ...) automatically stay inside the
margin and use writable-area coordinates - callers never add margin
offsets by hand, also not for getyx/getmaxyx/getbegyx and friends. The
private outer_window_ is the margin-inclusive BasicWindow; the border is
drawn on it, inside the margin. The margin is immutable: fixed when the
Window is constructed; changing it means creating a new Window.

A Window without margin creates no ncurses subwindow at all and must
behave exactly like a BasicWindow (decide how to then have exactly one
WINDOW* instead of both outer_window_ and an inset base).

Cases to work out here, documenting each chosen answer in the API
comments:

- Margin larger than the Window: top + bottom >= height or left + right
  >= width leaves no writable area. BasicWindow::subwin(Margin) already
  asserts `margin < size`; decide assert (programming error) vs clamp.
- Windows owned by ncurses itself (stdscr): decide whether the
  margin-aware Window can wrap those.
- Destruction order: C++ destroys the members (outer_window_) before
  the base (the InnerWindow subwindow), which is exactly the wrong way
  around - the subwindow must be delwin-ed before its parent. The
  Window destructor must enforce that explicitly (as must any
  operation that replaces either handle).
